WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.485
Welcome to Module 8 of the
Attribute-Driven Migration learning path:

2
00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:07.675
run the drift pipeline and
hand over the evidence.

3
00:00:07.675 --> 00:00:13.183
This module serves Gate 9, keep the
attributes true, and takes Chapter 09 of

4
00:00:13.183 --> 00:00:19.413
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration,
drift pipelines and evidence to the ISSO.

5
00:00:19.413 --> 00:00:23.814
You'll see where the pipeline sits, what
Learn's sample workflows leave to you,

6
00:00:23.814 --> 00:00:28.355
how one run is compared with the last
without treating a failed read as a fix, how

7
00:00:28.355 --> 00:00:30.730
findings go to the tracker without names,

8
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.175
and how the evidence
package forms a chain.

9
00:00:33.175 --> 00:00:37.900
Then you'll run it on your laptop against
two days of fictional validation kit runs.

10
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.137
If you're an ISSO reviewer,
this module is yours in full.

11
00:00:42.137 --> 00:00:45.337
The code is illustrative:
tested against fixtures,

12
00:00:45.337 --> 00:00:48.970
and the sample workflow has
never run against Azure.

13
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.270
Why does this module matter?

14
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.433
Without Gate 9, everything slowly decays.

15
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.480
Gate 9's Validate step runs the HR,

16
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.179
dynamic group and sync checks on the
drift cadence, and its evidence is each

17
00:01:04.179 --> 00:01:09.258
cycle's run folder, the findings queue
and the metrics for continuous monitoring.

18
00:01:09.258 --> 00:01:13.770
Each validation kit check already compares
the tenant with the program's record.

19
00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:18.170
The pipeline adds the second
comparison: this run against the last.

20
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.495
That finds change.

21
00:01:19.495 --> 00:01:24.632
In this module you run it on two days of
fixture runs, read what changed, and hand

22
00:01:24.632 --> 00:01:29.980
over a package the ISSO can check with
sha two fifty-six sum, and nothing else.

23
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.570
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

24
00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.217
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

25
00:01:39.217 --> 00:01:43.329
You'll explain what the pipeline adds
to the validation kit's own comparison.

26
00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:46.555
You'll describe what Learn's
sample workflows leave to you.

27
00:01:46.555 --> 00:01:50.255
You'll compare two runs
and read new, resolved,

28
00:01:50.255 --> 00:01:54.155
changed, detail only,
unchanged and unverified.

29
00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:58.355
You'll explain why a failed read
is unverified, not resolved.

30
00:01:58.355 --> 00:02:03.017
And you'll package and verify a run, and
explain how the packages form a chain.

31
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:04.917
Before you start, do Module 2:

32
00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:09.379
the pipeline reads the validation
kit's runs and their manifests.

33
00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:13.667
Modules 1, 3 and 5 produce
the provenance it packages.

34
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:18.074
Lab 1 needs Python 3.10 or
later with PyYAML and pytest,

35
00:02:18.074 --> 00:02:22.750
and a shell with sha two
fifty-six sum, such as Git Bash.

36
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.412
This workbook's own governance design
already says what this pipeline must produce.

37
00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:34.750
These are the workbook's rules,
not an external standard.

38
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.375
Drift is measured against a
versioned record, not a snapshot.

39
00:02:38.375 --> 00:02:41.187
No closure counts unless
it carries its proof.

40
00:02:41.187 --> 00:02:45.725
And a finding closes only when a later
observation shows the fixed state.

41
00:02:45.725 --> 00:02:48.287
The pipeline is a small
part of that design.

42
00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:52.312
Each validation kit check compares
the tenant with the program's record.

43
00:02:52.312 --> 00:02:56.275
The pipeline compares this run with
the last, and that finds change.

44
00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:59.925
Whether a change is drift from the
record is still the check's answer.

45
00:02:59.925 --> 00:03:02.769
A finding that was there
yesterday and is still

46
00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:05.175
there today isn't new,
but it is still open.

47
00:03:05.175 --> 00:03:08.598
And the comparison enforces
the closing rule in code:

48
00:03:08.598 --> 00:03:14.250
a check that ended in Error proves nothing,
so its findings stay open as unverified.

49
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.398
Microsoft Learn's page,
Use the Azure Login action

50
00:03:21.398 --> 00:03:24.713
with OpenID Connect, has
three sample workflows.

51
00:03:24.713 --> 00:03:30.137
They do the important part well: they sign
in with OpenID Connect and no secret,

52
00:03:30.137 --> 00:03:34.713
ask for id-token write and nothing
else, and keep the IDs in secrets.

53
00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:36.062
Keep that as the model.

54
00:03:36.062 --> 00:03:40.088
Here's what they leave to you, checked
offline by the kit's workflow linter.

55
00:03:40.088 --> 00:03:42.302
They run on push and have no schedule,

56
00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:45.550
and drift isn't found because
someone pushed a commit.

57
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:50.100
They pin actions by tag, and
version two is older than the

58
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.650
current release of Microsoft's
Azure Login action, v3.1.0.

59
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:58.298
GitHub Docs' secure use
reference advises pinning an

60
00:03:58.298 --> 00:04:01.863
action to a full commit SHA,
because a tag can be moved.

61
00:04:01.863 --> 00:04:04.669
They ask for the latest
Azure CLI and PowerShell,

62
00:04:04.669 --> 00:04:08.388
so the tool that produced the
evidence changes without a review.

63
00:04:08.388 --> 00:04:11.438
The US Government sample
doesn't parse as YAML.

64
00:04:11.438 --> 00:04:16.099
And the job names no environment, so the
token's subject depends on the branch.

65
00:04:16.099 --> 00:04:18.882
The verdict: use Learn's sign-in exactly,

66
00:04:18.882 --> 00:04:22.380
pin it, schedule it, and
put the rest behind it.

67
00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:30.705
Here's the comparison, and every rule
on this slide is the kit's own design.

68
00:04:30.705 --> 00:04:35.006
It reads each kit run and checks
it against the kit's own manifest:

69
00:04:35.006 --> 00:04:40.443
every listed file with the same hash, nothing
missing, nothing added, and read only.

70
00:04:40.443 --> 00:04:43.643
A run edited after the
kit wrote it is refused.

71
00:04:43.643 --> 00:04:49.063
It also refuses runs from different modes,
clouds or tenants, a run whose manifest

72
00:04:49.063 --> 00:04:53.755
has no tenant ID, and a current run
that isn't later than the previous one.

73
00:04:53.755 --> 00:04:59.867
Then it keys each Finding and Warn row by
check, object ID and issue, and sorts it.

74
00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:02.193
New is a row the last run didn't have.

75
00:05:02.193 --> 00:05:06.354
Changed is the same check and object
with a different severity or issue.

76
00:05:06.354 --> 00:05:11.030
Detail only is the same finding with
different detail, and opens nothing.

77
00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.355
Resolved is gone from a check
that read the tenant this run.

78
00:05:14.355 --> 00:05:19.580
And unverified is a check that ended in
Error or didn't run: its findings stay open.

79
00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.150
Each finding has a fingerprint, so
the tracker never opens a duplicate.

80
00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.812
Findings go to the issue tracker without
names; that's this workbook's guidance.

81
00:05:32.812 --> 00:05:36.133
A drafted issue holds
the check, the severity,

82
00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:39.363
the object ID, the issue
and the fingerprint.

83
00:05:39.363 --> 00:05:44.024
It leaves out the object's name, which
for most checks is a user principal name.

84
00:05:44.024 --> 00:05:47.512
The evidence store is
access-controlled and holds the names;

85
00:05:47.512 --> 00:05:50.650
the tracker is read by more
people and doesn't need them.

86
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:55.600
A first run is a baseline, so it
opens one summary issue, not hundreds.

87
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:56.775
Then the package.

88
00:05:56.775 --> 00:06:00.857
It holds the kit run as written,
the drift report and summary,

89
00:06:00.857 --> 00:06:05.272
the latest provenance from the
other kits, an evidence manifest,

90
00:06:05.272 --> 00:06:08.937
and a SHA256SUMS file that
sha two fifty-six sum reads.

91
00:06:08.937 --> 00:06:13.802
Each manifest records the previous
package's manifest hash, so the packages

92
00:06:13.802 --> 00:06:18.050
form a chain, and a replaced package
or a missing day shows as a break.

93
00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:19.762
The packager doesn't sign.

94
00:06:19.762 --> 00:06:24.984
Microsoft Learn's page on immutable
storage says a locked time-based retention

95
00:06:24.984 --> 00:06:27.436
policy lets blobs be created and read,

96
00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:31.920
but not modified or deleted,
until the retention interval ends.

97
00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:36.395
Try it.

98
00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:40.153
Lab 1 runs in the drift-pipeline
folder of the examples,

99
00:06:40.153 --> 00:06:42.658
in a POSIX shell, and nothing signs in.

100
00:06:42.658 --> 00:06:46.382
Step one checks Learn's three
sample workflows offline.

101
00:06:46.382 --> 00:06:51.433
Sample one has five findings and four
warnings, sample two has seven findings

102
00:06:51.433 --> 00:06:55.694
and four warnings, and sample three
has just one: it isn't valid YAML.

103
00:06:55.694 --> 00:06:57.233
The command exits two.

104
00:06:57.233 --> 00:07:02.545
Step two checks the pipeline's own
workflow: zero findings, zero warnings.

105
00:07:02.545 --> 00:07:04.920
Step three prints a cloud's hosts.

106
00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.867
For DoD, the sign-in authority is
login dot microsoftonline dot u s,

107
00:07:09.867 --> 00:07:13.439
the Graph host is dod-graph
dot microsoft dot u s,

108
00:07:13.439 --> 00:07:17.195
and the Azure environment
is Azure U S Government.

109
00:07:17.195 --> 00:07:22.744
Microsoft Learn's page, Microsoft
Graph national cloud deployments,

110
00:07:22.744 --> 00:07:25.957
lists those GCC High and DoD Graph hosts.

111
00:07:25.957 --> 00:07:30.526
That's the trap: the Azure environment
covers both GCC High and DoD,

112
00:07:30.526 --> 00:07:33.657
so the Graph host has to
come from the profile.

113
00:07:33.657 --> 00:07:36.478
Then ask for a name it
doesn't know, u s gov.

114
00:07:36.478 --> 00:07:40.980
It's refused, with exit code
one, never mapped to worldwide.

115
00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.643
Step four records a baseline.

116
00:07:46.643 --> 00:07:49.978
The fixtures are two
fictional validation kit runs,

117
00:07:49.978 --> 00:07:53.392
one day apart: October
twenty-eighth and twenty-ninth.

118
00:07:53.392 --> 00:07:56.690
Compare the first with
nothing, and it's a baseline:

119
00:07:56.690 --> 00:07:59.505
ten new findings, and
the command exits two.

120
00:07:59.505 --> 00:08:02.451
Package it with the
other kits' provenance,

121
00:08:02.451 --> 00:08:06.155
and the evidence manifest's
hash starts six d nine f f f.

122
00:08:06.155 --> 00:08:08.343
Step five compares the next day.

123
00:08:08.343 --> 00:08:13.087
The summary reads: new three,
resolved one, changed one,

124
00:08:13.087 --> 00:08:17.831
detail only one, unchanged
six, unverified checks two.

125
00:08:17.831 --> 00:08:20.018
Its table shows which checks moved.

126
00:08:20.018 --> 00:08:23.867
VK-CA-01 went from Pass to Fail.

127
00:08:23.867 --> 00:08:27.168
VK-PIV-01 didn't run.

128
00:08:27.168 --> 00:08:32.217
And VK-NPI-03 went from Fail to Error.

129
00:08:32.217 --> 00:08:33.718
Look closely at that one.

130
00:08:33.718 --> 00:08:35.555
Yesterday it had a finding.

131
00:08:35.555 --> 00:08:37.043
Today its read failed.

132
00:08:37.043 --> 00:08:40.017
So the finding is
unverified, not resolved.

133
00:08:40.017 --> 00:08:42.560
A failed read is never a fix.

134
00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:51.998
Step six drafts the findings: six
to open, two to close, no comments.

135
00:08:51.998 --> 00:08:53.572
Then search the drafts.

136
00:08:53.572 --> 00:08:59.210
For a user's address, grep counts zero;
the name isn't there, and grep exits one.

137
00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:01.685
For the object ID, it counts one.

138
00:09:01.685 --> 00:09:05.935
The tracker gets the object ID and
the fingerprint, never the name.

139
00:09:05.935 --> 00:09:08.118
Step seven packages the second day,

140
00:09:08.118 --> 00:09:12.560
chained to the first with the previous
manifest option, and verifies it.

141
00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:18.535
The manifest hash starts oh nine three
two, and verify reports sixteen files.

142
00:09:18.535 --> 00:09:22.608
Then the ISSO's own check,
with no program code at all:

143
00:09:22.608 --> 00:09:28.523
sha two fifty-six sum, in the package's
folder, prints nothing and exits zero.

144
00:09:28.523 --> 00:09:34.700
Last, read two fields of day two's manifest:
the previous package's manifest hash,

145
00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:40.211
which is day one's, six d nine f f f,
and the fixture tenant's placeholder ID.

146
00:09:40.211 --> 00:09:41.620
That's the chain.

147
00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:47.295
Step eight breaks it on purpose.

148
00:09:47.295 --> 00:09:51.345
Append one line to the packaged
drift summary, and verify again.

149
00:09:51.345 --> 00:09:55.995
It fails, with exit code one: the
summary doesn't match its hash.

150
00:09:55.995 --> 00:09:58.232
An edit after packaging can't hide.

151
00:09:58.232 --> 00:10:00.878
Then swap the runs, and
compare the twenty-ninth

152
00:10:00.878 --> 00:10:03.457
as previous with the
twenty-eighth as current.

153
00:10:03.457 --> 00:10:07.657
That's refused too: the current run
isn't later than the previous one.

154
00:10:07.657 --> 00:10:10.657
Runs out of order can't make
a finding look resolved.

155
00:10:10.657 --> 00:10:14.432
Step nine runs the tests:
one hundred forty-two pass.

156
00:10:14.432 --> 00:10:16.008
They take about two minutes.

157
00:10:16.008 --> 00:10:17.483
Step ten cleans up.

158
00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:22.260
Check your work: given two runs, you
can say what's new, resolved, changed or

159
00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:24.365
unverified, and given a package,

160
00:10:24.365 --> 00:10:28.360
you can show it's unedited
and chained to the day before.

161
00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:36.742
Lab 2 runs the pipeline for real, in
a test tenant and a test subscription,

162
00:10:36.742 --> 00:10:39.960
never production, from a
private test repository

163
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:43.022
that holds the validation
kit and this example.

164
00:10:43.022 --> 00:10:47.840
Register an app that trusts the repository
through a federated credential,

165
00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.898
so no secret is stored, and
grant it only the reads.

166
00:10:50.898 --> 00:10:54.568
Make an evidence container with
an unlocked retention policy,

167
00:10:54.568 --> 00:10:56.897
and set up the repository's environment.

168
00:10:56.897 --> 00:11:01.897
Run the workflow by hand for a baseline,
make one real change, and run it again.

169
00:11:01.897 --> 00:11:03.435
Then let the schedule run.

170
00:11:03.435 --> 00:11:07.747
To back it out, step eight deletes
the federated credential and the app.

171
00:11:07.747 --> 00:11:12.750
Leave the test retention policy unlocked:
Microsoft Learn's page on immutable

172
00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:16.752
storage says an unlocked policy
is only for short-term testing,

173
00:11:16.752 --> 00:11:18.060
and it can be deleted.

174
00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.210
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

175
00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:23.960
Follow Chapter 09's Lab 2, step by step.

176
00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.097
The federal requirement.

177
00:11:29.097 --> 00:11:35.111
The controls are from NIST SP
800-53, at NIST's Moderate baseline.

178
00:11:35.111 --> 00:11:38.156
This chapter turns the
other chapters' outputs

179
00:11:38.156 --> 00:11:41.123
into continuous monitoring,
control C A seven.

180
00:11:41.123 --> 00:11:45.511
The first run is a packaged
baseline, for baseline configuration.

181
00:11:45.511 --> 00:11:49.475
Each run says what is new,
resolved, changed and unverified,

182
00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:53.935
and a failed read never resolves
a finding, for system monitoring.

183
00:11:53.935 --> 00:11:57.448
Each finding is opened or
closed by fingerprint,

184
00:11:57.448 --> 00:12:00.698
with object IDs and no
names, for audit review.

185
00:12:00.698 --> 00:12:05.417
The package hashes every file, carries
the other kits' provenance and chains

186
00:12:05.417 --> 00:12:09.911
to the day before, for the protection
of audit information and integrity.

187
00:12:09.911 --> 00:12:14.835
An edit after packaging and runs out of
order are refused, and the tests show it.

188
00:12:14.835 --> 00:12:16.246
Every action is pinned,

189
00:12:16.246 --> 00:12:19.810
and the job that reads the tenant
can't write to the tracker.

190
00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:23.921
And in Lab 2, the pipeline signs
in with a federated credential,

191
00:12:23.921 --> 00:12:25.372
with no secret stored.

192
00:12:25.372 --> 00:12:28.884
The KSI column is from FedRAMP's
Key Security Indicators,

193
00:12:28.884 --> 00:12:32.022
which are indicators for
cloud service providers.

194
00:12:32.022 --> 00:12:35.950
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.

195
00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.675
Check yourself with two questions.

196
00:12:41.675 --> 00:12:46.563
First: yesterday VK-NPI-03 had a finding.

197
00:12:46.563 --> 00:12:48.987
Today its read failed
with a four-oh-three.

198
00:12:48.987 --> 00:12:50.776
What does the comparison report?

199
00:12:50.776 --> 00:12:54.262
The answer is b: unverified, not resolved.

200
00:12:54.262 --> 00:12:58.688
A failed read is never a fix, and
you saw it in step five of the lab.

201
00:12:58.688 --> 00:13:03.413
Second: how does the ISSO check a
package without the program's code?

202
00:13:03.413 --> 00:13:07.315
The answer is b: with
sha two fifty-six sum,

203
00:13:07.315 --> 00:13:11.337
dash c, SHA256SUMS, in
the package's folder.

204
00:13:11.337 --> 00:13:16.125
You did exactly that in step seven,
and it printed nothing and exited zero.

205
00:13:16.125 --> 00:13:20.662
The module page has two more questions,
on what goes into a drafted issue

206
00:13:20.662 --> 00:13:25.650
and on what shows a missing day, each
linked to the section that answers it.

207
00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:30.225
To sum up.

208
00:13:30.225 --> 00:13:35.385
You compared two days of kit runs without
letting a failed read close a finding,

209
00:13:35.385 --> 00:13:39.751
drafted findings without names,
and packaged and verified a chain

210
00:13:39.751 --> 00:13:43.800
of evidence the ISSO can check
with sha two fifty-six sum alone.

211
00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:49.138
The evidence an ISSO keeps: each day's
package, with its kit run, drift report,

212
00:13:49.138 --> 00:13:53.163
the other kits' provenance,
the manifest and SHA256SUMS,

213
00:13:53.163 --> 00:13:58.588
in a container whose retention is locked
in production; the findings in the

214
00:13:58.588 --> 00:14:04.538
tracker by fingerprint; and the workflow
file and the pipeline app's registration.

215
00:14:04.538 --> 00:14:09.013
Gate 9 adds the scan schedule, the
limits register and the metrics.

216
00:14:09.013 --> 00:14:12.725
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 09 checklist.

217
00:14:12.725 --> 00:14:16.717
Next is Module 9, the last:
deploy to test, then production,

218
00:14:16.717 --> 00:14:21.577
with one settings file per stage, a
pre-flight check, a smoke test that

219
00:14:21.577 --> 00:14:26.400
says go or no-go, and a rollback
written from a record of every change.
